How the py script for serializing Blender models to json is to be used

The engine converts Blender coordinates as:
	engine = glm::vec3(blender.x, blender.z, -blender.y);

You should check the initial config values at the top of the script everytime before running it for a specific room.

Change this per room: ROOM_ID = [some name]
Examples:
   ROOM_ID = "testRoom"
   ROOM_ID = "Room01"
   ROOM_ID = "room02"

Also change CAMERA_NAMES:
e.g CAMERA_NAMES = ["Cam01", "Cam02"]
make sure to follow the format of two digits and start from 01


Set ROOM_WALLS_OBJECT to match the mesh used to calculate outer wall bounds.
e.g ROOM_WALLS_OBJECT = "room_walls"

To add lights in a room:
1. Add point light: Add -> Light -> Point
2. Rename it, e.g Light_01
3. Position it 
4. Add custom properties, e.g:
	color = 1.0, 0.75, 0.45
	intensity = 4.0
	radius = 14.0
	flicker = false
(if you do not add custom properties the script will refer to default values)

To set the ambient color in a room:
1. Go to Scene and add a custom property (can be a float array)
2. Name it "ambientColor"
3. Set the values
(if you do not add custom ambient color the script will use default values)


Naming convention in Blender for my game engine:
Required:
	PlayerSpawn
	
	Cameras:
		Cam[number e.g 0]_Pos
		Cam[number e.g 0]_Target
		Cam[number e.g 0]_Zone
	Colliders:
		Any mesh object whose name starts wih Col_:
			e.g Col_Wall_Left, Col_Table, Col_Blocker_01
	Doors:
		Door_01
		Door_Hallway
		Door_01_Spawn and etc.

	Interactions:
		Interact_TestNote
		Interact_WallMessage
		Interact_* and etc.
	
	Lights:
		Light_01
		Light_02
		Light_Hallway

How to set door target room (fov) in Blender:
	Select door cube/object and:
		Object properties -> Custom properties -> Add
	Add:
		targetRoom (fov)
	Value example:
		src/models/room02/room02.json
	The script shall output: "targetRoom": "src/models/room02/room02.json"

